        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#capacity-myth" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Why the Weight Capacity Figure Misleads</a>
          <a href="#criteria" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The Five Specs That Actually Matter Here</a>
          <a href="#how-we-tested" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. How We Tested at Weight</a>
          <a href="#picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. The Six Best Treadmills for Heavy Users</a>
          <a href="#warranty" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Warranty Terms Deserve a Careful Read</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Maintenance Matters More at Higher Loads</a>
          <a href="#walking-first" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. A Note on Starting With Walking</a>
          <a href="#delivery" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Delivery, Floors and Getting It Into the Room</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Final Recommendations</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A 350 lb weight capacity does not mean the machine is comfortable at 340 lbs. It means
          the manufacturer will honour the warranty up to that figure. Those are different
          claims, and the gap between them is where most disappointing purchases in this
          category happen.
        </p>

        <div id="capacity-myth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why the Weight Capacity Figure Misleads</h2>
<p>
            Every treadmill lists a maximum user weight, and almost everyone shopping in this
            category treats it as the primary specification. It is worth understanding what that
            number actually is: a warranty boundary chosen by the manufacturer's legal and
            engineering teams together, above which they will not honour a claim. It is not a
            point at which the machine fails, and it is not a promise that the machine performs
            well anywhere below it.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, a machine rated to 300 lbs is engineered around a user meaningfully
            lighter than that. Run it near its ceiling and several things change at once: the
            deck deflects further on each footfall so the cushioning bottoms out sooner, the
            motor draws more current and runs hotter, the belt experiences more friction against
            the deck, and the rollers take a larger load across a smaller contact area. None of
            those produce an immediate failure. All of them shorten the machine's life and make
            it less pleasant to use from day one.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical rule we would give anyone shopping here is to treat the stated capacity
            as though it were 50 to 75 lbs lower than printed. If you weigh 280 lbs, shop
            machines rated 350 lbs and above rather than machines rated 300. That single
            adjustment does more for your experience and the machine's lifespan than any other
            decision on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="criteria" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Five Specs That Actually Matter Here</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Roller diameter.</strong> The most informative and least advertised
            specification in this category. Rollers are the cylinders the belt runs around, and
            their diameter determines how tightly the belt has to flex on every rotation and how
            much load the bearings carry. Budget machines use 1.9-inch rollers; better ones use
            2.5 inches; commercial machines use 3 inches or more. Larger rollers mean less belt
            wear, less bearing load and less motor strain — and under a heavier user, all three
            matter considerably more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor type and continuous rating.</strong> A DC motor under sustained heavy
            load builds heat, and heat is what kills treadmill motors. AC motors, which appear on
            commercial machines, handle continuous load far better. If you are buying a DC
            machine, 3.5 CHP is a sensible floor rather than a nice extra. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains the continuous-versus-peak distinction that matters most here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame mass and deck support.</strong> How many points support the deck, and
            how heavy is the frame? A deck supported at four points flexes between them; a deck
            on a dense support structure does not. Assembled weight is again the best available
            proxy, and in this category we would not look below 250 lbs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck width.</strong> Twenty-two inches rather than twenty. This is a comfort
            and safety specification for heavier users specifically: a wider stance is common,
            and a belt that does not accommodate it means rail strikes and constant small
            corrections.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cushioning recovery, not softness.</strong> The question is not how soft the
            deck feels but whether it returns to shape between footfalls under a heavy load. A
            deck that compresses and stays compressed is one that has run out of travel, and that
            is when you start feeling impact through the frame.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Tested at Weight</h2>
<p>
            Testing this category honestly requires testers at the weights involved. Our primary
            tester for this guide is 6'1" and 285 lbs; a second tester at 315 lbs used every
            machine for at least two weeks. Where we needed to assess behaviour closer to a
            machine's stated ceiling, we added plate weight in a carried vest up to 330 lbs for
            controlled walking sessions only, never for running.
          </p>
          <p>
            Each machine ran a minimum of six weeks of mixed walking and jogging. We measured
            deck deflection under static load at the centre of the belt, logged motor housing
            temperature after 45 minutes of continuous walking at 3.5 mph on a 5% grade, checked
            belt tracking every ten miles, and recorded frame movement at the console during
            jogging.
          </p>
          <p>
            The motor temperature test was the most revealing and produced the clearest
            separation in the group. Two machines that felt entirely adequate to walk on were
            running hot enough after 45 minutes that we would not have wanted to repeat the
            session daily for a year. That is the kind of problem a showroom visit cannot
            surface, and it is the single most common reason machines in this category fail
            early.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Six Best Treadmills for Heavy Users</h2>
<p>
            Capacities below are the manufacturers' stated figures. Our recommendations assume
            the 50 to 75 lb margin described above, and each entry says who we would and would
            not put on the machine.
          </p>
        </div>
