      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Warranty Terms Deserve a Careful Read</h2>
<p>
            Warranty terms matter more in this category than in any other, and they are worth
            reading in full rather than skimming the headline number. Two clauses are worth
            finding specifically.
          </p>
          <p>
            The first is whether the warranty is voided by exceeding the stated capacity. Almost
            all of them are, and that is reasonable, but it makes the capacity figure a financial
            matter rather than a comfort one. If two people in a household will use the machine
            and one is above the limit, the warranty position needs to be understood before
            purchase, not after a failure.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second is the distinction between parts, labour and the frame. Frame warranties
            of ten years or lifetime look generous and cover the component least likely to fail.
            What you want to see is a long parts warranty and, ideally, more than one year of
            labour, because the expensive failures in this category are motors and controller
            boards and the labour to replace them is a significant share of the cost. Our
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifespan and repair guide</a>
            covers the repair-or-replace arithmetic in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Maintenance Matters More at Higher Loads</h2>
<p>
            Everything in our standard maintenance schedule applies here on a shorter interval.
            Friction between the belt and the deck is the primary cause of motor and controller
            failure, and load increases friction directly — so a heavier user generates the
            failure condition faster.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where our general advice is to lubricate every 40 to 50 hours of use, in this
            category we would treat 30 hours as the interval, and we would check belt tension and
            tracking monthly rather than quarterly. That is perhaps twenty minutes of work a year
            more than a lighter user needs, against a motor replacement that costs several
            hundred dollars.
          </p>
          <p>
            The step-by-step is in our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication guide</a>,
            and the full schedule is in the
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance pillar</a>.
            One warning worth repeating: check your manual before applying anything, because some
            decks are pre-waxed and are ruined by silicone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. A Note on Starting With Walking</h2>
<p>
            This is an equipment guide rather than a training one, but one point is worth making
            because it changes which machine to buy. Impact force scales with body weight, and
            running generates roughly two and a half to three times body weight per footfall
            against approximately one and a half times for walking. At 300 lbs that is a very
            large difference in load, both on your joints and on the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Many people shopping in this category will get better results, and a much easier
            first three months, from incline walking than from running — more work done, far less
            impact, and a machine that will last longer for it. If that is the likely use, the
            deck length requirement relaxes and the money is better spent on frame and motor. Our
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking treadmill guide</a>
            and the
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 incline protocol</a>
            both cover that approach, and the protocol page includes a gentler build-up. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-weight-loss/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmills and weight loss</a>
            is honest about what training alone does and does not achieve.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Delivery, Floors and Getting It Into the Room</h2>
<p>
            The machines that suit this category are the heavy ones, and assembled weights of
            250 to 400 lbs create practical problems that are easy to discover too late. Almost
            every treadmill is delivered as kerbside freight by default, which means a pallet on
            your driveway and nothing more. Threshold and room-of-choice delivery are usually
            available as paid upgrades, and for a machine in this weight class they are worth
            buying rather than improvising with friends.
          </p>
          <p>
            Measure the route before you order, not just the room. Doorway widths, stairwell
            turns and any landing pivot are where deliveries fail, and a treadmill that arrives
            in one crate cannot be angled through a tight turn the way flat-packed furniture can.
            If the machine is going upstairs, confirm with the retailer whether their delivery
            team will carry it up — many will not above a stated weight, regardless of what you
            have paid for.
          </p>
          <p>
            Floor loading is worth a moment's thought too, though it is rarely a genuine problem.
            A 350 lb machine plus a 300 lb user is 650 lbs spread across four feet, which is
            comparable to a loaded bookcase and well within normal residential floor capacity.
            What does matter is what the machine sits on: a solid, level surface rather than a
            thick carpet, and a proper mat underneath to spread load and stop the frame walking
            under use. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions and space guide</a>
            covers the delivery route and clearance requirements in full, and
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">placing a treadmill on carpet</a>
            explains why carpet is a problem for the motor as well as for stability.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Final Recommendations</h2>
<p>
            If the budget allows it, the <strong>Life Fitness T3</strong> is the right answer and
            it is not close. A commercial AC motor, a 400 lb capacity that is genuinely
            engineered rather than nominal, and a frame that does not move are exactly what this
            use case needs, and it is the one machine here we would put a 330 lb user on without
            reservation.
          </p>
          <p>
            At half that money, the <strong>Sole F65</strong> is the value pick: 325 lbs stated,
            heavier construction than its price suggests, and no subscription. For a user up to
            about 260 lbs it is entirely sufficient. The <strong>NordicTrack Commercial 1750</strong>
            is the choice if you want a large screen and guided content alongside a frame that
            can take the load.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever arrives, halve the lubrication interval and check tension monthly. In this
            category maintenance is not housekeeping — it is the difference between six years and
            two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What weight capacity treadmill do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Shop 50 to 75 lbs above your actual weight. The stated capacity is a warranty boundary rather than a performance guarantee, and machines run near their ceiling deflect further, run hotter and wear faster. If you weigh 280 lbs, look at machines rated 350 lbs and above rather than 300.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you exceed a treadmill weight limit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Nothing dramatic and nothing good. The immediate effects are increased deck deflection, more belt-to-deck friction and a hotter motor, all of which shorten the machine's life rather than stopping it. The more immediate consequence is that almost every manufacturer voids the warranty above the stated figure, so a motor or board failure becomes entirely your cost.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is roller diameter really that important?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a heavier user, yes — it is the most informative specification that manufacturers rarely advertise. Larger rollers flex the belt less tightly on each rotation, spin more slowly for the same belt speed and spread bearing load better. Budget machines use 1.9-inch rollers, good ones 2.5 inches, commercial machines 3 inches or more, and the difference shows up in belt and bearing life.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should a heavier beginner run or walk on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Walking, and particularly incline walking, is the better starting point for most people. Running generates roughly two and a half to three times body weight per footfall against about one and a half times for walking, which at higher body weights is a very large difference for both your joints and the machine. Incline walking delivers most of the cardiovascular benefit at close to half the load.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a heavy user lubricate a treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly every 30 hours of use rather than the 40 to 50 hours we recommend generally, because load increases belt-to-deck friction directly and friction is what kills motors and controller boards. Check belt tension and tracking monthly too. Always confirm against your manual first — some decks are pre-waxed and must never have silicone applied.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are treadmills with higher weight capacities always better built?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, but check the supporting specifications rather than trusting the headline. A high stated capacity alongside 1.9-inch rollers, a sub-3.0 CHP motor and a light assembled weight is a marketing figure rather than an engineering one. Capacity, roller diameter, motor rating and assembled weight should tell a consistent story; when they do not, believe the other three.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying for a Heavier User</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">These four account for most of the disappointing purchases in this category, and the first one causes the other three.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the stated capacity as a performance figure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It is a warranty boundary, not an engineering limit, and machines operating near it deflect further, run hotter and wear faster from the first session. Shop 50 to 75 lbs above your actual weight. This single adjustment does more for both your experience and the machine's lifespan than anything else on this page.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never checking roller diameter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It is the specification that most affects belt and bearing life under load, and it is almost never advertised. A 1.9-inch roller flexes the belt tightly on every rotation and spins fast to move the belt at all. Find the figure before buying — if a retailer cannot tell you, that is itself informative.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a DC motor for long daily incline sessions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">DC motors build heat under sustained load and heat is the primary failure mechanism. If you plan 45-minute incline walks most days at a higher body weight, an AC motor is a genuinely different class of component and worth the price step. If sessions will be shorter and flatter, a well-rated DC machine is fine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Keeping the standard maintenance interval</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Higher load means more belt-to-deck friction, and friction is what destroys motors and controller boards. Halve the lubrication interval to roughly 30 hours and check tension and tracking monthly. That is about twenty extra minutes a year against a motor replacement costing several hundred dollars.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

